---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import SubNav from '../../components/SubNav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Pokemon GBA ROM Hacking Guide - Boink Brands"
  description="A beginner to intermediate guide to Pokemon GBA ROM hacking using the modern decompilation workflow, covering hardware, toolchain setup, Porymap, Poryscript, and a structured learning curriculum."
>
  <Header />
  <SubNav activeTab="3D Art" />

  <section class="bg-void relative overflow-hidden">
    <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-[radial-gradient(ellipse_at_top,rgba(230,0,18,0.18)_0%,transparent_62%)]"></div>
    <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-[linear-gradient(90deg,rgba(255,255,255,0.02)_1px,transparent_1px)] bg-[size:36px_36px] opacity-20"></div>
    <div class="relative max-w-[var(--width-content)] mx-auto px-4 py-12 md:py-16">
      <p class="text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-accent mb-3">Interactive Guide</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl md:text-6xl uppercase leading-none tracking-tight mb-4">
        <span class="text-gradient">GBA ROM Hacking</span>
        <br />
        <span class="text-text-primary">Beginner to Intermediate</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="text-text-secondary text-sm md:text-base max-w-3xl leading-relaxed">
        The ecosystem of Game Boy Advance Pokemon ROM hacking has evolved into a sophisticated software development
        landscape. This guide covers the modern decompilation workflow exclusively, from GBA hardware architecture
        to map editing, scripting, and a structured four-week learning curriculum.
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-3 mt-8 max-w-4xl">
        <div class="guide-stat">
          <p class="guide-stat-label">Workflow</p>
          <p class="guide-stat-value">pokeemerald-expansion decompilation</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-stat">
          <p class="guide-stat-label">Toolchain</p>
          <p class="guide-stat-value">WSL, devkitARM, Porymap, Poryscript</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-stat">
          <p class="guide-stat-label">Curriculum</p>
          <p class="guide-stat-value">Four-week milestone-based plan</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="chrome-divider"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-obsidian py-8 md:py-10">
    <div class="max-w-[var(--width-content)] mx-auto px-4">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-[260px_1fr] gap-6">
        <aside class="surface-card rounded-sm p-3 lg:sticky lg:top-6 lg:self-start h-fit">
          <p class="text-[10px] uppercase tracking-widest text-text-muted font-bold px-3 pb-2">Sections</p>
          <nav id="guide-nav" class="space-y-1">
            <a href="#hardware" class="guide-nav-link is-active">GBA Hardware</a>
            <a href="#workflow" class="guide-nav-link">Decompilation Workflow</a>
            <a href="#maps" class="guide-nav-link">Map Creation</a>
            <a href="#npcs" class="guide-nav-link">NPC Placement</a>
            <a href="#scripting" class="guide-nav-link">Scripted Events</a>
            <a href="#battles" class="guide-nav-link">Trainer Battles</a>
            <a href="#starters" class="guide-nav-link">Starter Pokemon</a>
            <a href="#items" class="guide-nav-link">Item Editing</a>
            <a href="#encounters" class="guide-nav-link">Wild Encounters</a>
            <a href="#sprites" class="guide-nav-link">Custom Sprites</a>
            <a href="#curriculum" class="guide-nav-link">Learning Curriculum</a>
            <a href="#mistakes" class="guide-nav-link">Common Mistakes</a>
          </nav>

          <div class="mt-5 pt-4 border-t border-border space-y-3">
            <div class="guide-side-note">
              <p class="guide-side-note-label">Base Engine</p>
              <p class="guide-side-note-copy">pokeemerald-expansion by RHH with Gen 9 mechanics and modern battle engine.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="guide-side-note">
              <p class="guide-side-note-label">Format</p>
              <p class="guide-side-note-copy">Step-by-step tutorials with code samples, memory tables, and progress checkpoints.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </aside>

        <main id="guide-content" class="space-y-6">

          <!-- GBA Hardware -->
          <section id="hardware" data-guide-section class="surface-card rounded-sm p-6 md:p-8 scroll-mt-24">
            <p class="section-kicker">Architecture</p>
            <h2 class="font-display text-3xl md:text-4xl uppercase text-text-primary leading-none mb-4">GBA Hardware and Memory</h2>
            <p class="text-sm md:text-base text-text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-6">
              The Game Boy Advance is a 32-bit system powered by an ARM7TDMI processor at 16.78 MHz. It relies on a
              rigid, hardcoded memory map. While the decompilation compiler handles most allocation automatically,
              understanding this map is crucial for debugging.
            </p>

            <div class="surface-card rounded-sm p-5 md:p-6 bg-charcoal/30 overflow-x-auto">
              <h3 class="font-display text-xl uppercase text-text-primary mb-4">Memory Map</h3>
              <table class="guide-table">
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th>Sector</th>
                    <th>Size</th>
                    <th>Address Range</th>
                    <th>Function</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr>
                    <td><strong>BIOS</strong></td>
                    <td>16 KB</td>
                    <td><code>0x00000000 - 0x00003FFF</code></td>
                    <td>Read-only system instructions provided by Nintendo.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td><strong>On-Chip WRAM</strong></td>
                    <td>32 KB</td>
                    <td><code>0x03000000 - 0x03007FFF</code></td>
                    <td>Fast Work RAM embedded directly on the CPU.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td><strong>On-Board WRAM</strong></td>
                    <td>256 KB</td>
                    <td><code>0x02000000 - 0x0203FFFF</code></td>
                    <td>Slower Work RAM. Stores the active game state.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td><strong>VRAM</strong></td>
                    <td>96 KB</td>
                    <td><code>0x06000000 - 0x06017FFF</code></td>
                    <td>Video RAM. Stores graphical tile data.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td><strong>OAM</strong></td>
                    <td>1 KB</td>
                    <td><code>0x07000000 - 0x070003FF</code></td>
                    <td>Object Attribute Memory. Tracks up to 128 hardware sprites.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td><strong>PAL RAM</strong></td>
                    <td>1 KB</td>
                    <td><code>0x05000000 - 0x050003FF</code></td>
                    <td>Palette RAM. Stores 512 distinct 15-bit colors.</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr>
                    <td><strong>ROM</strong></td>
                    <td>Up to 32 MB</td>
                    <td><code>0x08000000 - 0x09FFFFFF</code></td>
                    <td>Read-Only Memory. The actual game cartridge data.</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <div class="border-l-2 border-accent bg-accent/10 p-4 mt-6 rounded-sm">
              <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
                <strong class="text-text-primary">Key takeaway:</strong> In traditional binary hacking, developers manually managed pointers and free space.
                In the decompilation workflow, the C compiler automatically allocates memory and assigns pointers during the build process,
                rendering manual hex editing obsolete.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- Decompilation Workflow -->
          <section id="workflow" data-guide-section class="surface-card rounded-sm p-6 md:p-8 scroll-mt-24">
            <p class="section-kicker">Modern Workflow</p>
            <h2 class="font-display text-3xl md:text-4xl uppercase text-text-primary leading-none mb-4">The Decompilation Paradigm</h2>
            <p class="text-sm md:text-base text-text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-6">
              The Pokemon Reverse Engineering Team (pret) successfully reverse-engineered the original games back into highly
              readable, modular C code. Rather than modifying a pre-compiled binary, you modify raw C source files, JSON data
              structures, and standard PNG image assets, then compile a new .gba ROM from scratch.
            </p>

            <div class="surface-card rounded-sm p-5 md:p-6 bg-charcoal/30 mb-6">
              <p class="mini-kicker">Recommended Base</p>
              <h3 class="font-display text-xl uppercase text-text-primary mb-3">pokeemerald-expansion</h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
                The undisputed standard in 2026 is the pokeemerald-expansion repository maintained by the Rom Hacking Hideout (RHH).
                This heavily modified fork acts as a modern game engine with Generation 9 Pokemon, updated abilities, modernized
                battle mechanics, and modern items natively integrated.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="surface-card rounded-sm p-5 md:p-6 bg-charcoal/30">
              <h3 class="font-display text-xl uppercase text-text-primary mb-2">Essential Toolchain</h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-4">Click items as you install them. This keeps dependencies in order and prevents setup failures later.</p>
              <ul id="toolchain-checklist" class="space-y-3">
                <li class="checklist-item" data-checklist-item>
                  <span class="checkmark">[ ]</span>
                  <span><strong>WSL (Ubuntu)</strong> — Required to run the Linux-based compiler on Windows. Install via <code>wsl --install -d Ubuntu</code>.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="checklist-item" data-checklist-item>
                  <span class="checkmark">[ ]</span>
                  <span><strong>devkitARM</strong> — The compiler toolchain. Install via devkitPro pacman inside your WSL terminal.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="checklist-item" data-checklist-item>
                  <span class="checkmark">[ ]</span>
                  <span><strong>Visual Studio Code</strong> — The recommended text editor for editing C files and Poryscript.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="checklist-item" data-checklist-item>
                  <span class="checkmark">[ ]</span>
                  <span><strong>Poryscript Extension</strong> — VS Code extension for autocomplete and syntax highlighting.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="checklist-item" data-checklist-item>
                  <span class="checkmark">[ ]</span>
                  <span><strong>GitHub Desktop</strong> — Used to clone the source code and manage versions.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="checklist-item" data-checklist-item>
                  <span class="checkmark">[ ]</span>
                  <span><strong>pokeemerald-expansion</strong> — Clone the repo via <code>git clone</code> in your WSL terminal.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="checklist-item" data-checklist-item>
                  <span class="checkmark">[ ]</span>
                  <span><strong>Porymap</strong> — The exclusive visual map editor for decompilation projects.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="checklist-item" data-checklist-item>
                  <span class="checkmark">[ ]</span>
                  <span><strong>mGBA</strong> — The definitive, highly accurate GBA emulator for testing compiled ROMs.</span>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="border-l-2 border-accent bg-accent/10 p-4 mt-6 rounded-sm">
              <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
                <strong class="text-text-primary">Compiling:</strong> Open your WSL terminal, navigate to your pokeemerald-expansion
                folder, and run <code>make -jX</code> (where X is your CPU core count, e.g. <code>make -j8</code>).
                This generates a <code>pokeemerald.gba</code> file you can open in mGBA.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- Map Creation -->
          <section id="maps" data-guide-section class="surface-card rounded-sm p-6 md:p-8 scroll-mt-24">
            <p class="section-kicker">Tutorial 1</p>
            <h2 class="font-display text-3xl md:text-4xl uppercase text-text-primary leading-none mb-4">Map Creation and Terrain Modification</h2>
            <p class="text-sm md:text-base text-text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-6">
              Porymap is a standalone desktop application that reads your project folders and visualizes the map data.
              It handles metatile layout (8x8 pixel blocks grouped into 16x16 game tiles) and automatically generates
              collision boundaries based on the tiles you select.
            </p>

            <div class="surface-card rounded-sm p-5 md:p-6 bg-charcoal/30">
              <h3 class="font-display text-xl uppercase text-text-primary mb-4">Step-by-Step Process</h3>
              <ul class="space-y-3">
                <li class="checklist-item" data-checklist-item>
                  <span class="checkmark">[ ]</span>
                  <span>Launch Porymap and go to <strong>File &gt; Open Project</strong>. Select your pokeemerald-expansion root folder.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="checklist-item" data-checklist-item>
                  <span class="checkmark">[ ]</span>
                  <span>On the left sidebar, expand <strong>data &gt; maps</strong> and click on <strong>Route101</strong>.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="checklist-item" data-checklist-item>
                  <span class="checkmark">[ ]</span>
                  <span>In the center you will see the layout. On the right sidebar is your Blockset (the palette of available terrain tiles).</span>
                </li>
                <li class="checklist-item" data-checklist-item>
                  <span class="checkmark">[ ]</span>
                  <span>Click a grass tile in the Blockset, then click and drag on the central map to paint over existing terrain.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="checklist-item" data-checklist-item>
                  <span class="checkmark">[ ]</span>
                  <span>Switch to the <strong>Collisions</strong> tab. Porymap auto-assigns Passable (blue) or Impassable (red) values. You can manually override these.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="checklist-item" data-checklist-item>
                  <span class="checkmark">[ ]</span>
                  <span>Click <strong>File &gt; Save</strong> (Ctrl+S).</span>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="guide-checkpoint">
              <p class="guide-checkpoint-label">Progress Checkpoint</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
                Compile your ROM with <code>make</code> and open mGBA. Walk out of Littleroot Town into Route 101.
                You should instantly see your new terrain layout, and the game will correctly block you from walking through any trees you placed.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- NPC Placement -->
          <section id="npcs" data-guide-section class="surface-card rounded-sm p-6 md:p-8 scroll-mt-24">
            <p class="section-kicker">Tutorial 2</p>
            <h2 class="font-display text-3xl md:text-4xl uppercase text-text-primary leading-none mb-4">NPC Placement and Spatial Events</h2>
            <p class="text-sm md:text-base text-text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-6">
              Beyond terrain, maps need objects. Porymap uses the Events tab to inject coordinates for NPCs, item balls,
              and warp doors directly into the map&apos;s JSON data.
            </p>

            <div class="surface-card rounded-sm p-5 md:p-6 bg-charcoal/30">
              <h3 class="font-display text-xl uppercase text-text-primary mb-4">Step-by-Step Process</h3>
              <ul class="space-y-3">
                <li class="checklist-item" data-checklist-item>
                  <span class="checkmark">[ ]</span>
                  <span>In Porymap (still on Route 101), click the <strong>Events</strong> tab at the top.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="checklist-item" data-checklist-item>
                  <span class="checkmark">[ ]</span>
                  <span>Right-click on an empty grass tile and select <strong>Add Object Event</strong>. A new NPC placeholder will appear.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="checklist-item" data-checklist-item>
                  <span class="checkmark">[ ]</span>
                  <span>In the Properties panel, change the <strong>Graphics</strong> dropdown to <code>OBJ_EVENT_GFX_FAT_MAN</code>.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="checklist-item" data-checklist-item>
                  <span class="checkmark">[ ]</span>
                  <span>Set his <strong>Movement Type</strong> to <code>MOVEMENT_TYPE_LOOK_AROUND</code>.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="checklist-item" data-checklist-item>
                  <span class="checkmark">[ ]</span>
                  <span>In the <strong>Script</strong> text box, type: <code>Route101_EventScript_FatMan</code>.</span>
                </li>
                <li class="checklist-item" data-checklist-item>
                  <span class="checkmark">[ ]</span>
                  <span>Save the map in Porymap.</span>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="guide-checkpoint">
              <p class="guide-checkpoint-label">Progress Checkpoint</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
                Compile and open mGBA. Walk to the spot where you placed the NPC. The Fat Man will be there, looking around.
                If you press A to talk to him, nothing happens yet because we haven&apos;t written his script.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- Scripting -->
          <section id="scripting" data-guide-section class="surface-card rounded-sm p-6 md:p-8 scroll-mt-24">
            <p class="section-kicker">Tutorial 3</p>
            <h2 class="font-display text-3xl md:text-4xl uppercase text-text-primary leading-none mb-4">Scripted Events and Interactive Dialogue</h2>
            <p class="text-sm md:text-base text-text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-6">
              Poryscript is a programming language designed specifically for Pokemon decomp hacking. It allows you to write
              C-style code (using if/else statements and loops) that the compiler translates into Game Boy assembly.
            </p>

            <div class="surface-card rounded-sm p-5 md:p-6 bg-charcoal/30 mb-6">
              <p class="mini-kicker">Example Script</p>
              <h3 class="font-display text-xl uppercase text-text-primary mb-3">NPC Dialogue</h3>
              <pre class="guide-code-block"><code>script Route101_EventScript_FatMan {'{'}
    lock
    faceplayer
    msgbox(format("Hello, {'{'}{`PLAYER`}{'}'}! Welcome to my newly remodeled Route 101!"))
    release
    end
{'}'}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
              <article class="surface-card rounded-sm p-5 md:p-6 bg-charcoal/30">
                <p class="mini-kicker">Command Reference</p>
                <ul class="guide-list">
                  <li><strong>lock:</strong> Stops the NPC and surrounding NPCs from wandering off while you speak.</li>
                  <li><strong>faceplayer:</strong> Forces the NPC to turn and look at the player.</li>
                  <li><strong>release / end:</strong> Restores the game to normal overworld state and terminates the script.</li>
                </ul>
              </article>

              <article class="surface-card rounded-sm p-5 md:p-6 bg-charcoal/30">
                <p class="mini-kicker">Key Feature</p>
                <ul class="guide-list">
                  <li><strong>msgbox(format("...")):</strong> The msgbox command calls the text UI window.</li>
                  <li><strong>format() wrapper:</strong> Poryscript&apos;s greatest feature, automatically calculating line breaks for the GBA screen so text never overflows the box.</li>
                </ul>
              </article>
            </div>

            <div class="guide-checkpoint">
              <p class="guide-checkpoint-label">Progress Checkpoint</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
                Save the <code>.pory</code> file and run <code>make</code>. Because you only changed a script, compilation takes seconds.
                In mGBA, talk to the Fat Man. A text box will appear with perfectly formatted dialogue.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- Trainer Battles -->
          <section id="battles" data-guide-section class="surface-card rounded-sm p-6 md:p-8 scroll-mt-24">
            <p class="section-kicker">Tutorial 4</p>
            <h2 class="font-display text-3xl md:text-4xl uppercase text-text-primary leading-none mb-4">Trainer Battle Configuration</h2>
            <p class="text-sm md:text-base text-text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-6">
              Triggering combat uses a built-in macro called <code>trainerbattle_single</code>. The engine handles the music
              change, screen transition, and saving the victory state automatically.
            </p>

            <div class="surface-card rounded-sm p-5 md:p-6 bg-charcoal/30 mb-6">
              <p class="mini-kicker">Example Script</p>
              <h3 class="font-display text-xl uppercase text-text-primary mb-3">Trainer Battle</h3>
              <pre class="guide-code-block"><code>script Route101_EventScript_NewTrainer {'{'}
    trainerbattle_single(TRAINER_CALVIN_1, "Halt! Let's battle!", "Wow, you are strong!")
    msgbox(format("I need to go train some more before I challenge the Gym."), MSGBOX_AUTOCLOSE)
    end
{'}'}</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="border-l-2 border-accent bg-accent/10 p-4 rounded-sm">
              <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
                <strong class="text-text-primary">How it works:</strong> When you talk to the NPC, <code>trainerbattle_single</code>
                checks a hidden flag tied to <code>TRAINER_CALVIN_1</code>. If the flag is 0 (undefeated), it displays the first
                string, fights you, displays the second string upon losing, and sets the flag to 1. If the flag is already 1,
                the engine skips the battle and plays the <code>msgbox</code> at the bottom instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="guide-checkpoint">
              <p class="guide-checkpoint-label">Progress Checkpoint</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
                Compile and test. Walk in front of the new NPC to be pulled into a battle against Bug Catcher Calvin.
                Win, talk to him again, and verify he only says his post-battle dialogue.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- Starter Pokemon -->
          <section id="starters" data-guide-section class="surface-card rounded-sm p-6 md:p-8 scroll-mt-24">
            <p class="section-kicker">Tutorial 5</p>
            <h2 class="font-display text-3xl md:text-4xl uppercase text-text-primary leading-none mb-4">Modifying Starter Pokemon Selections</h2>
            <p class="text-sm md:text-base text-text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-6">
              To change a starter, you must modify both the mechanical script that gives the player the Pokemon
              and the visual C arrays that dictate what sprite appears on the briefcase UI.
            </p>

            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
              <article class="surface-card rounded-sm p-5 md:p-6 bg-charcoal/30">
                <p class="mini-kicker">Step 1: The Logic</p>
                <h3 class="font-display text-xl uppercase text-text-primary mb-3">Script Modification</h3>
                <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
                  Navigate to <code>data/maps/Route101/scripts.pory</code>. Find <code>Route101_EventScript_ChooseStarter</code>.
                  Locate the three <code>givemon</code> commands and change <code>givemon(SPECIES_MUDKIP, 5, ITEM_NONE)</code>
                  to <code>givemon(SPECIES_SQUIRTLE, 5, ITEM_NONE)</code>.
                </p>
              </article>

              <article class="surface-card rounded-sm p-5 md:p-6 bg-charcoal/30">
                <p class="mini-kicker">Step 2: The Visuals</p>
                <h3 class="font-display text-xl uppercase text-text-primary mb-3">Sprite Array Update</h3>
                <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
                  Open <code>src/starter_choose.c</code>. Search for <code>SPECIES_MUDKIP</code> in the <code>sStarterMon</code>
                  array and change it to <code>SPECIES_SQUIRTLE</code>.
                </p>
              </article>
            </div>

            <div class="guide-checkpoint">
              <p class="guide-checkpoint-label">Progress Checkpoint</p>
              <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
                Compile your ROM. Start a new game, trigger the Professor Birch event, and open the briefcase.
                The right-most Poke Ball will display a Squirtle sprite, and selecting it adds a Level 5 Squirtle to your party.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- Item Editing -->
          <section id="items" data-guide-section class="surface-card rounded-sm p-6 md:p-8 scroll-mt-24">
            <p class="section-kicker">Tutorial 6</p>
            <h2 class="font-display text-3xl md:text-4xl uppercase text-text-primary leading-none mb-4">Item Editing and Database Management</h2>
            <p class="text-sm md:text-base text-text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-6">
              The decomp project stores global data like stats, moves, and items in centralized <code>.h</code> header files.
              Changes here propagate across the entire game.
            </p>

            <div class="surface-card rounded-sm p-5 md:p-6 bg-charcoal/30 mb-6">
              <p class="mini-kicker">Example: Potion Price Change</p>
              <h3 class="font-display text-xl uppercase text-text-primary mb-3">gItemsInfo Array</h3>
              <pre class="guide-code-block"><code>{` =
{
    .name = _("POTION"),
    .price = 10,          // Changed from 300
    .description = sPotionDesc,
    .pocket = POCKET_ITEMS,
